﻿Discussion
-----------

### ﻿Taxonomic history

Writing of the subfamily __Leptanillinae__, [Brown (1954](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B27): 28) opined that “ … it is doubtful that we shall ever be certain of its true affinities.” Concomitantly, the classification of the __Leptanillinae__ relative to other __Formicidae__ has a convoluted history. Extreme morphological derivation (in males, larvae, and both female castes), varying markedly across the few lineages of the clade, is responsible for this.

For most of its taxonomic history, the subfamily __Leptanillinae__ was subsumed within ([Emery 1910](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B41)), or affiliated with, the army ants (__Dorylinae___sensu_ Ashmead) ([Baroni Urbani 1989](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B5); [Hölldobler and Wilson 1990](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B49)), with ___Leptanilla___ having been described within the __Dorylinae__ ([Emery 1870](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B39)). Despite ill-interrogated placement in the __Myrmicinae__ by many early authors ([Emery and Forel 1879](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B42); [Dalla Torre 1893](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B34); [Ashmead 1905](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B1); [Emery 1910](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B41)), the description of dichthadiiform gynes in ___Leptanilla___ was interpreted as supporting its placement within the __Dorylinae__ ([Emery 1904](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B40)), while [Santschi (1907)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B78) asserted the similarity of putative male ___Leptanilla___ to male army ants. [Wheeler (1923)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B96) was the first to elevate the then-monobasic __Leptanillini__ to subfamily rank, an action also argued for by [Wheeler (1928)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B97) and [Wheeler and Wheeler (1965)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B100) due to the dissimilarity of the larval habitus between the __Dorylinae__ and __Leptanillinae__. ___Leptanilloides___ (__Dorylinae__) was placed as __Formicidae__ incertae sedis and likened to the __Leptanillinae__ by [Borgmeier (1955)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B17) due to that genus exhibiting “a mixture of characters of the __Ecitonini__ (i.e., New World army ants) and __Leptanillinae__” ([Borgmeier 1955](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B17): 652), but [Brown (1975](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B28): 34) classified ___Leptanilloides___ within the “doryline section” ([Bolton 1990a](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B12)) due to its close resemblance to ___Sphinctomyrmex____sensu lato_, a classification followed by all subsequent authors and confirmed by phylogenetic inference from molecular data (e.g., [Brady et al. 2014](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B25)).

With the description of the tribe __Anomalomyrmini__ within the __Leptanillinae__, [Bolton (1990b](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B13): 267) “dispute(d) the indisputability” of leptanilline kinship with army ants, since ___Protanilla___ gynes are not dichthadiiform ([Baroni Urbani and de Andrade 2006](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B6); [Billen et al. 2013](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B10); [Hsu et al. 2017](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B51)), and dichthadiigynes are unequivocally homoplasious in their other occurrences across the __Formicidae__ ([Bolton 1990a](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B12)). [Bolton (1990b)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B13) transferred ___Apomyrma___ to the __Leptanillinae__ from the __Ponerinae___sensu_[Bolton (1990b)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B13) and proposed that the resemblance of doryline to leptanilline gynes was homoplasious. Based on the theorized kinship of ___Apomyrma___ to the __Leptanillinae__ (__Apomyrminae__ and __Leptanillinae__ constituting the “leptanillomorph subfamilies” _sensu_[Bolton (2003)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B14)), these lineages were hypothesized to have affinity with the __Amblyoponinae__, or more generally the “poneroid” clade ([Ward 2007](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B91)).

The advent of molecular sequencing supported none of the above hypotheses: instead, __Leptanillinae__ was consistently supported as an early-diverging lineage of the __Formicidae__ not akin to ___Apomyrma___, which was recovered as a poneroid, sister to the __Amblyoponinae__. In addition, [Ward and Fisher (2016)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B94) robustly recovered the monotypic genus ___Opamyrma___, which had been described within the __Amblyoponinae__ on account of character states closely resembling those of ___Apomyrma___ (e.g., abdominal sternite II reduced), as sister to the remaining __Leptanillinae__ ([Ward and Fisher 2016](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B94)). This inference is corroborated by male morphology.

The __Leptanillinae__ have been afflicted by a dual taxonomy since the description of the first putative males by [Santschi (1907](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B78), [1908](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B79)). The first males of ___Leptanilla___ were described without association with workers, justified by purported similarity in head morphology, and “only with some doubt (n’est qu’avec doute)” ([Santschi 1907](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B78): 312). The genus ___Phaulomyrma___ was erected for ___Leptanilla__javana___ (Wheeler & Wheeler, 1930) and ___Leptanilla__tanit___ Santschi, 1907, both known only from males ([Wheeler and Wheeler 1930](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B99)), whereas the bizarre monotypic genus ___Scyphodon___, described by [Brues (1925)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B31) as __Hymenoptera__ incertae sedis, was found to represent a male leptanilline ([Petersen 1968](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B73); [Boudinot 2015](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B20)), although [Ogata et al. (1995)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B71) argued against the placement of ___Scyphodon___ in the __Formicidae__. The genera ___Noonilla___ and ___Yavnella___ were also described in the __Leptanillinae__ based solely upon male specimens ([Petersen 1968](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B73); [Kugler 1987](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B58)). [Ogata et al. (1995)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B71) was the first to associate male and worker leptanilline specimens, describing the male of ___Leptanilla__japonica___, which was previously known from workers ([Baroni Urbani 1977](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B4)), and confirming the hypothesis of [Santschi (1907)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B78). The two genera for which the tribe __Anomalomyrmini__ was established were each initially known only from workers (___Protanilla___) or gynes (___Anomalomyrma___) ([Bolton 1990b](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B13)). Consideration of morphology illuminated by phylogenetic inference ([Borowiec et al. 2019](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B19); [Griebenow 2020](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B43), [2021](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B44); [Griebenow et al. 2022](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B45)) demonstrates a lack of reciprocal monophyly, and the two are here synonymized. Males were only subsequently associated with ___Protanilla___ (namely the ___Protanilla__rafflesi___ species group) by means of phylogenomic inference ([Griebenow 2020](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B43)). The __Opamyrmini__ have avoided comparable taxonomic problems, with the collection of the male of ___O.__hungvuong___ in association with females ([Yamada et al. 2020](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B106)).

### ﻿Biogeography and ecology

The __Leptanillinae__ are, as per the 95% credibility interval inferred for the crown age of this clade by [Borowiec et al. (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B19), no older than the beginning of the Cenozoic Era (66 mya). The crown age of the __Leptanillinae__ is no older than the estimated origins of several ant clades that have a circumtropical or cosmopolitan distribution, including ___Odontomachus___ (__Ponerinae__: __Ponerini__) ([Schmidt 2013](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B82)) and ___Camponotus___ (__Formicinae__: __Camponotini__) ([Blaimer et al. 2015](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B11)). Yet, curiously, the __Leptanillinae__ are restricted to the Old World. The bulk of leptanilline diversity resides in the humid tropics, with the few temperate lineages (e.g., ___Leptanilla__taiwanensis___; [Man et al. 2017](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B65)) being close kin of tropical ones. This implies that the origin of the __Leptanillinae__ occurred in tropical climates, conforming to the overall tendency observed in the __Formicidae__ ([Economo et al. 2018](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B38)). In the absence of other data to explain the absence of this clade from the New World, I predict that leptanilline ants originated after the closure of the Thulean and Beringian land bridges to tropical biota, but this prediction remains to be tested.

The notable absence of the __Leptanillinae__ from the Neotropics elicits inquiry into which ants occupy a similar ecological niche in this ecoregion. In terms of functional morphology and behavior, ___Leptanilloides___ differs from leptanilline ants in the presence of cincti on abdominal segments IV–VII and in being an obligate predator of ant brood, rather than hunting geophilomorph centipedes; despite their name, these minute dorylines are not a Neotropical analog to the __Leptanillinae__. Rather, it is probable that centipede predators such as ___Prionopelta___ and ___Fulakora___ (__Amblyoponinae__), which often display LHF ([Ito and Billen 1998](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B53)), are ecological counterparts to the __Leptanillinae__ in the New World. This hypothesis is further supported by remarkable homoplasy between the __Amblyoponinae__ and __Leptanillinae__, which resulted in the erroneous hypothesis that these clades were akin ([Bolton 1990b](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B13), [2003](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B14)).

___Typhlomyrmex___ (__Ectatomminae__: __Ectatommini__), which are minute hypogaeic ants precinctive to the Neotropics, are also worth noting here on account of the leptanilloid gestalt of the worker. Coarse but pronounced resemblance in habitus implies functional parallels in ___Typhlomyrmex___ with the __Leptanillinae__, with the articulated meso-metapleural suture that is unique to ___Typhlomyrmex___ among the __Ectatomminae__ ([Bolton 2003](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B14)) recalling that feature in ___Protanilla___ and certain ___Leptanilla___ species, while the tergosternal fusion of abdominal segment II constitutes convergence with the __Leptanillini__. Miniaturized and flexible relative to the robust, epigaeic members of their sister clade, ___Gnamptogenys____sensu stricto_ ([Camacho et al. 2022](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10838183/#B32)), ___Typhlomyrmex___ represent __Ectatomminae__ that occupy a morphospace occupied outside the New World by the __Leptanillinae__.

